        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Climbing a ramp"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "A ramp rises 3 m over a horizontal length of 4 m. The ratio of rise to run tells us how steep it is - and the idea of a ratio of sides is the start of trigonometry."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "opposite, adjacent, hypotenuse",
          "sin cos tan",
          "depend on the angle only",
          "special angles",
          "60 degrees",
          "complementary",
          "identity"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "In a right triangle, with respect to an angle A, the sides are opposite, adjacent and hypotenuse.\nsin A = opposite / hypotenuse, cos A = adjacent / hypotenuse, tan A = opposite / adjacent.\nThe ratios depend only on the angle, not on the size of the triangle.\nSpecial angles: sin 30 = 1/2, cos 30 = root 3 / 2, tan 30 = 1/root 3; for 45 degrees sin = cos = 1/root 2 and tan = 1.\nFor 60 degrees: sin 60 = root 3 / 2, cos 60 = 1/2, tan 60 = root 3.\nComplementary angles: sin (90 - A) = cos A and cos (90 - A) = sin A.\nsin squared A + cos squared A = 1."
        }
      },
